  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe Westerlies remain displaced north of the area with a quasi-zonal \nflow pattern holding across the central Plains through much of the \nperiod. The stationary boundary draped across the CWA, essentially \nthrough Brown County back through Manhattan and then into central \nKansas, continues to serve as the focus for both the heat gradient \ntoday and storm chances tonight.\n\nFor the remainder of this afternoon, cloud cover remains the \ndeciding factor on heat. Lingering debris clouds have kept most \nnorth central areas from approaching extreme heat criteria, and the \nwarning has been cancelled there. East-central and portions of \nnortheast Kansas still have the best chance at heat index values up \nto around 105-108 degrees if late day breaks in the cloud cover \nallow temperatures to recover generally along and southeast of the \nboundary.\n\nStorms congeal over eastern Colorado into western Kansas this \nevening and work east across the area overnight, supported by a weak \nconvectively enhanced vort max aloft and weak isentropic ascent \nalong the 310K surface after midnight into the early morning hours. \nThe primary LLJ focuses southeast of the area and veers into the \novernight, which argues against sustained upscale growth locally. \nPoor mid-level lapse rates and a more stable boundary layer with \neastward extent should limit efficient momentum transfer from cold \npools to the surface, allowing updrafts to become less robust as \nstorms progress east. The exception appears to be along and south of \nthe stationary boundary, where sufficient DCAPE lingers. If storms \ntake a more southerly track, damaging winds and heavy rain would \nremain possible mainly across southern portions of the forecast area \nbefore activity fully weakens and shifts east early Sunday morning.\n\nBehind the departing boundary, surface high pressure expands south \ninto the area Monday, trimming both temperatures and dewpoints with \nhighs mostly in the 80s Sunday and Monday. How long the relief lasts \nis the main question for the upcoming week, as the boundary wavers \nback north into Tuesday and ensemble temperature spreads remain wide \nthrough midweek. Heat index values in the 90s look common even in \nthe cooler scenarios. Periodic shower and storm chances continue \nwith the quasi-zonal regime, but unless a more amplified trough can \ndig this far south, confidence in any single period remains low and \nPOPs hold around 30 percent or lower through the long range.\nThe upper pattern suggests another western ridge amplifies into next \nweekend, which would open the door to heat rebuilding across the \nregion.",
